Thanks for the report. Monarch evaluates rules against the remaining substring, so after consuming the prefix of QdStop, the rule sees only Stop. A leading word boundary or negative lookbehind therefore cannot inspect the consumed prefix.

For your examples, match the complete word first and classify it using keyword cases:

```javascript
monaco.languages.setMonarchTokensProvider('mySpecialLanguage', {
  keywords: ['Stop'],
  tokenizer: {
    root: [
      [/\w+/, { cases: { '@keywords': 'keyword', '@default': '' } }]
    ]
  }
});
```

This highlights standalone Stop while leaving QdStop, 11Stop, and the suffixed examples unstyled. This is the documented configuration pattern rather than a new editor defect; the same matching behavior is explained at https://github.com/microsoft/monaco-editor/issues/890#issuecomment-392540348.

Further usage questions belong on StackOverflow's monaco-editor tag: https://stackoverflow.com/questions/tagged/monaco-editor.

Thanks for the report. `autoSurround: 'never'` disables wrapping selected text with quotes or brackets; it does not disable inserting a matching closing character while typing. To disable both behaviors, use:

```js
myEditor.updateOptions({
  autoSurround: 'never',
  autoClosingQuotes: 'never',
  autoClosingBrackets: 'never'
});
```

This is a configuration question rather than an editor defect. For further usage questions, please use https://stackoverflow.com/questions/tagged/monaco-editor.

Thanks for the request. You can already enable this through the TypeScript defaults; the compiler-options index signature accepts the flag even though it is not listed as a named property:

```ts
const defaults = monaco.languages.typescript.typescriptDefaults;
defaults.setCompilerOptions({
  ...defaults.getCompilerOptions(),
  strictNullChecks: true,
  exactOptionalPropertyTypes: true
});
```

The bundled TypeScript language service receives and honors this option, so no new Monaco capability is needed. Configuration questions can go to https://stackoverflow.com/questions/tagged/monaco-editor.

Thanks for the report. The typed language entry points were added in https://github.com/microsoft/monaco-editor/commit/558bbbcc1619da88d466bd64856f337a5604e43c and shipped in v0.56.0. For a minimal bundle, use:

```ts
import * as monaco from 'monaco-editor/editor';
import { typescriptDefaults } from 'monaco-editor/languages/features/typescript/register';

typescriptDefaults.setDiagnosticsOptions(diagnosticsOptions);
```

The editor-only entry still does not export language support; the separate TypeScript entry now exposes the defaults with their declarations. This resolves configuring TypeScript without loading the all-inclusive entry.

Thanks for the report and the migration examples. Typed individual language entry points shipped in v0.56.0 through https://github.com/microsoft/monaco-editor/commit/558bbbcc1619da88d466bd64856f337a5604e43c. You do not need to register every language to access TypeScript defaults:

```ts
import * as monaco from 'monaco-editor/editor';
import * as typescript from 'monaco-editor/languages/features/typescript/register';

typescript.typescriptDefaults.setExtraLibs([]);
```

Editor features, language definitions and workers can be configured separately as needed. This resolves the typed minimal-import problem; the older documentation and sample paths noted here still deserve a separate update.
